ruleResultT -- what if and while read when a rule is called from an action body: a match with a value, a match with an
empty capture, and a failure; and reading a piece by its label afterwards. Both grammars, pinned as read (Clay's dispatch,
2026-11-19; the ruling is Tony's, from these rows).

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each under an alarm: everything above this
file's stop(), then on the OLD road (incant/grammar) a call of the row's action; on the NEW road (kantGrammar) rrNew on
it, then the call. A sentinel on stderr ends each. Each row reads `stdout | R<REFUSED lines> x<exit>` and the sentinel.

The rule rv is [ then an OPTIONAL val then ]: on "[ab]" it matches with a value, on "[]" it matches with val empty (the
val term is absent and close is there), on "ab" it fails.
rs spells the empty case with a star on a set, val^=[a-z]* -- and a star on a set needs at least one character, so on
"[]" rs FAILS (RR-9). That is the spelling an empty CSV cell would reach for first, and it does not match empty.

MEASURED 2026-11-19, the same on both grammars:
  RR-1, RR-2  if rule(x) and if t = rule(x): value true, EMPTY TRUE, fail false.
  RR-3        if t := rule(x): value true, empty true, and FAIL TRUE -- the condition reads the holder t, which is always
              there, not the match. Not what anyone means; pinned as read.
  RR-4        while t = rule(x) on the same field: a match loops (capped at 3 by a break), a failure runs no pass.
  RR-5        after if t = rule(x), *t.val REFUSES ("the left of this dot holds nothing"): = made t the result, not a holder.
  RR-6        after if t := rule(x), *t.val reads ab.
  RR-10       after if t = rule(x), the unstarred t.val reads ab.
  RR-11       t := rule(x); then if *t: value true, empty true, fail false.
  SO TWO SPELLINGS ARE CONSISTENT: with =, no stars (if t = rule(x); ... t.val); with :=, stars throughout
  (t := rule(x); if *t; ... *t.val). Mixing them is the trap (RR-3, RR-5).
  RR-7        after an empty match close reads ] (it matched); the absent val reads as a tag echo, no refusal.
  RR-8        after a failure, reading a piece refuses by name ("the left of this dot holds nothing").
  RR-9        rs: value true, empty false (the set-star does not match zero characters).

